WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.228
Picture a Friday evening.

2
00:00:04.228 --> 00:00:10.585
A payments app has added wallets, so friends can send each other money by a short name, called a handle.

3
00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:14.365
Two people named Ana sign up in the same millisecond.

4
00:00:14.365 --> 00:00:18.325
Both ask for the handle @ana, and both are told it's theirs.

5
00:00:18.567 --> 00:00:24.551
On Saturday a friend sends 20.00 to @ana for concert tickets, and it lands with the wrong Ana.

6
00:00:24.551 --> 00:00:28.116
Somewhere, the other Ana is having a very nice Saturday.

7
00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:31.053
So how can two people both be told yes?

8
00:00:31.053 --> 00:00:32.379
Hold that question.

9
00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:34.474
The sign-up code looks right.

10
00:00:34.474 --> 00:00:37.838
Check whether @ana is taken, and if it isn't, save it.

11
00:00:37.838 --> 00:00:42.948
It reads like the rule, and it passes every test that sends one sign-up at a time.

12
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.391
The app runs on many app servers: computers that each run a copy of the program.

13
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:53.665
Handles live in a database, a program that keeps records as rows in a table.

14
00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:56.401
The two sign-ups reach two servers.

15
00:00:56.401 --> 00:00:59.716
Each asks the database: is there a row for ana?

16
00:00:59.716 --> 00:01:00.844
Each finds none.

17
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.966
So each one saves a row.

18
00:01:02.966 --> 00:01:04.676
Two rows now say @ana.

19
00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:06.952
Why didn't the database stop it?

20
00:01:06.952 --> 00:01:10.117
It makes a second writer wait on a row that exists.

21
00:01:10.117 --> 00:01:13.157
Here there was no row yet, so nothing to wait on.

22
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.602
Tests are polite.

23
00:01:14.602 --> 00:01:15.733
They take turns.

24
00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:16.581
Users don't.

25
00:01:16.581 --> 00:01:20.610
Two servers each said yes without hearing from the other.

26
00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:25.700
What we want is one place that hears both requests, one after the other.

27
00:01:25.967 --> 00:01:30.261
A theatre box office has this problem every night, and an old answer.

28
00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:36.111
Every request for Friday's show goes to the one clerk who holds Friday's seating chart.

29
00:01:36.111 --> 00:01:38.099
She handles them one at a time.

30
00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:41.339
The first request for seat F12 gets it.

31
00:01:41.339 --> 00:01:43.777
The second is told: sorry, sold.

32
00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:44.982
Let's map it.

33
00:01:44.982 --> 00:01:51.035
The clerk is a processor: a program that reads requests in order and decides each one.

34
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.528
Her seating chart is its table of taken handles.

35
00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:00.247
And the queue at her window is a log: a list of records that only ever grows at the end.

36
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:05.669
The simplest version: one log, and one processor that does one thing at a time.

37
00:02:05.933 --> 00:02:09.975
The first claim for @ana wins, and later ones are rejected.

38
00:02:09.975 --> 00:02:11.003
That's correct.

39
00:02:11.233 --> 00:02:14.705
But now every claim in the whole app waits in one queue.

40
00:02:14.705 --> 00:02:18.301
That's one clerk selling seats for every show in the city.

41
00:02:18.301 --> 00:02:19.851
Very fair, and very slow.

42
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:28.533
In the lesson's simulation of 2,000 claims, one claim in a hundred waited 5,785 steps or longer.

43
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.789
The box office grows by nights, not by seats.

44
00:02:31.789 --> 00:02:34.644
Friday's seats never clash with Saturday's.

45
00:02:34.644 --> 00:02:38.164
And claims for @bob never clash with claims for @ana.

46
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.012
So split the log into partitions: separate logs, each with its own processor.

47
00:02:44.012 --> 00:02:44.813
Here, four.

48
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.605
A hash of the handle picks the partition.

49
00:02:47.605 --> 00:02:52.431
A hash is a number worked out from the text, always the same for the same text.

50
00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.158
The hash of ana is 96,724.

51
00:02:55.158 --> 00:02:58.846
Divide by four, and the remainder is 0.

52
00:02:58.846 --> 00:03:05.371
So user 17's claim goes to partition 0, at offset 0, the first place.

53
00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:10.958
User 43's claim names the same handle, so it lands right behind it, at offset 1.

54
00:03:10.958 --> 00:03:15.043
Whatever the phones' clocks say, the log has fixed the order.

55
00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.637
Processor 0 reads them in order.

56
00:03:17.637 --> 00:03:19.463
What does it say to each?

57
00:03:19.463 --> 00:03:20.632
Pause and guess.

58
00:03:23.867 --> 00:03:29.911
Offset 0: ana is free, so it's noted as user 17's, and the claim is granted.

59
00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:33.859
Offset 1: ana is taken, so user 43 is told no.

60
00:03:33.859 --> 00:03:37.391
Nothing else can change processor 0's table.

61
00:03:37.633 --> 00:03:43.827
Meanwhile @bob hashes to partition 1, and processor 1 decides it at the same moment.

62
00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:54.475
With eight partitions, the slow tail fell from 5,785 steps to 2,274, and no handle was given out twice.

63
00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:56.822
Now let's break it on purpose.

64
00:03:56.822 --> 00:03:59.956
Say someone routes claims by user ID instead.

65
00:03:59.956 --> 00:04:01.000
It sounds tidy.

66
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.786
Where do the two claims for @ana go now?

67
00:04:03.786 --> 00:04:04.900
Pause and guess.

68
00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:13.064
User 17 hashes to partition 2, and user 43 to partition 3.

69
00:04:13.064 --> 00:04:16.040
Two empty tables, and both say yes.

70
00:04:16.267 --> 00:04:18.927
Nothing failed, and nothing was logged.

71
00:04:18.927 --> 00:04:20.973
And @ana has two owners again.

72
00:04:21.233 --> 00:04:28.232
That's two clerks, each with a copy of Friday's chart, both politely selling seat F12.

73
00:04:28.232 --> 00:04:31.487
The simulation counted 937 extra owners.

74
00:04:31.487 --> 00:04:34.416
The plain check, then save, gave 31.

75
00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:38.701
So the value that picks the partition is called the partition key.

76
00:04:38.701 --> 00:04:42.430
And for a rule like this one, the partition key is the rule.

77
00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:46.780
Every claim for one handle must meet one decider, one after the other.

78
00:04:47.033 --> 00:04:49.627
The box office picture breaks at a crash.

79
00:04:49.627 --> 00:04:52.094
A clerk who faints still has her chart.

80
00:04:52.094 --> 00:04:54.435
A crashed processor loses its memory.

81
00:04:54.733 --> 00:05:00.967
It reads its partition again from offset 0 and decides the same way, in the same order.

82
00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:03.045
So ana goes to user 17 again.

83
00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.506
Each claim carries a request ID, one per tap.

84
00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:13.489
If user 43's claim arrives again, the processor finds that ID among its answers and says no again.

85
00:05:13.733 --> 00:05:16.346
So, how were two people both told yes?

86
00:05:16.346 --> 00:05:18.890
Two deciders, each sure it was alone.

87
00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:26.660
The fix is one decider per handle: a partition keyed by the handle, where one processor says yes or no, in order.

88
00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.039
One quick check.

89
00:05:28.039 --> 00:05:31.102
Two people ask for @leo at the same moment.

90
00:05:31.102 --> 00:05:34.022
Which processor decides, and who gets it?

91
00:05:34.022 --> 00:05:35.161
Pause and think.

92
00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.305
Leo hashes to partition 2.

93
00:05:40.305 --> 00:05:46.093
Processor 2 decides both, and the claim that landed first in the log gets @leo.
